# Findings `make scan-alpha` accepts in the alpha server's third-party images, and why. Only that target reads this
# file (--ignorefile), so the API and gateway scans still report the same IDs if they ever show up there. The form is
# the root .trivyignore's (docs/security/dependencies.md 3장): the reason above each ID and an expiry, after which the
# scan fails again and the finding is looked at anew.
#
# caddy:2.11.7 is the newest Caddy image (built 2026-10-21 with Go 1.26.8 and golang.org/x/net 0.59.0). When a Caddy
# image built with Go 1.26.10 or later and x/net 0.60.0 or later is out, bump it, delete these lines and turn HTTP/2
# back on in the Caddyfile.

# HTTP/2 frame flood (Go standard library and x/net). The alpha Caddy serves HTTP/1.1 only until then (Caddyfile).
CVE-2026-78669 exp:2026-12-04
# Range header handling of Go's file serving. The alpha Caddy only proxies to the API and serves no files.
CVE-2026-78667 exp:2026-12-04
# ECH outer extensions in a ClientHello. Go reads them only when ECH keys are configured, and the Caddyfile sets none.
CVE-2026-97031 exp:2026-12-04
# Certificate chains Caddy verifies as a TLS client. Its one such connection is ACME renewal with Let's Encrypt across
# the internet, so someone on that path could at worst make a renewal fail or forge an ACME answer; the API behind
# Caddy is plain HTTP on the compose network. This premise is the weakest of the seven: bump Caddy before the expiry.
CVE-2026-111943 exp:2026-12-04
# go-jose's JWK parsing. In Caddy it backs the ACME client, which signs with its own key; nothing a visitor sends
# reaches it.
CVE-2026-116587 exp:2026-12-04
# Alpine's brotli-libs and curl in the image. Nothing in the container runs them: Caddy is a static Go binary.
CVE-2026-111497 exp:2026-12-04
CVE-2026-92392 exp:2026-12-04
